<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roskilde</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roskilde is a city on Zealand, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, at the head of the Roskilde Fjord. It was a royal and episcopal centre from the late tenth century, and its cathedral, built from the twelfth century, has been the burial place of Danish monarchs since the fifteenth and is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> ships found in the fjord are displayed in its Viking Ship Museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roskilde.jpg" alt="Roskilde" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nico/ Nils Jepsen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RD_fra_bispegaarden.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Roskilde lies in north-central Zealand at the south end of Roskilde Fjord, the south branch of the Isefjord. The city centre around the cathedral stands about 40 metres above sea level and 1,300 metres south of the fjord, and on the slope down to the harbour there are several springs, the strongest of which is Maglekilde. Saxo Grammaticus and other early sources associate the name, meaning Ro's spring, with King Roar, who possibly lived there in the 6th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Roskilde developed as a hub of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> land and sea trade routes more than a thousand years ago. From the 11th century until 1443 it was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, and with the support of kings and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> it became one of the most important centres in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>. Adam von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> called it Zealand's largest town in 1070. Five Viking ships of the 11th century were scuttled in the fjord to block a channel and protect the city from seaborne attack.</p><p>The cathedral was built in the 12th and 13th centuries on the site of a 10th-century wooden church and completed in 1275. It was the first Gothic cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> built of brick, a style that spread through northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in 1536 the town had some 6,000 inhabitants, but war, fire and disease reduced them to only 1,550 by 1753. Fires in the 18th century led to the rebuilding of most of the old town, although the original street plan survives. The first train from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> arrived on 26 June 1847.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Roskilde is a city 30 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>. Its cathedral, where 39 Danish monarchs are buried, has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1995. Next to it stands Roskilde Palace, built from 1733 to 1736 on the site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>' residence. The Viking Ship Museum beside the fjord displays the remains of the five ships, excavated in the late 1960s about 20 kilometres north of the city. Roskilde University was founded in 1972, and the Roskilde Festival, a rock music festival, has been held annually since 1971.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roskilde” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Towns such as Ribe, <b>Roskilde</b>, Lund and later Copenhagen were small by continental standards, and the Hanseatic League dominated much of their trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Skuldelev ships, sunk as a barrier in <b>Roskilde</b> Fjord in Denmark and raised between 1957 and 1962, include a warship some 30 metres long built near Dublin about 1042 and a sturdy cargo ship from western Norway.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li></ul></section>
